  "mechanism": "Extending the existing 64x64 page-aligned split path to INT8 lets the paged attention work over more independent KV segments while reusing the kernel's INT32 QK accumulation, explicit QK/V scales, BF16 partial state, and softmax-aware reducer. The trace showed the candidate paged kernel with lower LDS use (8 KiB) and VGPR use (176) than the reference grouped path (32 KiB and 256 VGPR).",
  "applicability": "Hygon BW/gfx936, HY3 INT8 paged prefill, page size 64, NHD/HND, Q=512 and KV=4096/32768 under the existing metadata-based guard: unmasked or sparsity bucket 2, supported KV layout, at least two default Q tiles, and default CTA count below the existing device-wave threshold.",
  "transferable_lesson": "When the paged Triton kernel and reducer already support INT8 scaling and partial-state merging, a guarded page-aligned split can be shared across BF16 and INT8 without changing the attention math. Keep the original fallback for short Q, other page sizes/layouts, and unvalidated sparsity classes; benchmark each precision separately.",
  "limitations": "Measured on one BW/gfx936 environment with batch 1, 32 query heads, 4 KV heads, 64-token pages, Q lengths 127/512 and KV lengths 127/4096/32768. All 12 tested rows improved with a 1.3506x geomean latency speedup. Longer contexts, batch sizes above one, other page sizes, scales beyond benchmark defaults, and other sparsity buckets remain unvalidated. Random-block FA comparison uses an approximate window mask, not the identical mask.",
